WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.417
Imagine one hundred price updates arrive. The app counts one hundred. The database shows zero. Can we tick the box that says all arrived?

00:00:08.417 --> 00:00:18.250
The old audit could say yes because another counter showed one hundred. The fix marks the gap as unverified and checks again. When both counts reach one hundred, they agree.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:25.833
We sent two audit receipts to a real test database, then sent the same two again. After a restart we counted two rows.

00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:34.667
All seven fields we checked matched the original values. This tells us what survived in the test. A live feed needs its own record of which updates arrived.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:45.750
Picture a frame as an envelope holding four price updates. We timed sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-six envelopes on each of three paths. The line marks one microsecond.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:55.750
At the ninety-ninth percentile, all three paths crossed the line. The standard path took about one point eight microseconds. Both full paths took more than three point seven.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:01:04.583
The paper order step took zero point seven five one microseconds at the ninety-ninth percentile. It ran inside the app with the order book ready.

00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:14.917
The step that builds a labelled database line took one point three four two microseconds. Timing a full database round trip or a live broker confirmation needs a separate clock.

00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:27.000
Imagine highlighting each line you read. Local tests highlighted all sixty-one changed lines of production Rust. Twenty were in the count reader, forty in the dashboard, and one in the audit decision.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:37.917
Across the whole project, the exact main build reached eighty-four point one nine percent of production lines. Reading a line once leaves room for other inputs to behave differently.

00:01:37.917 --> 00:01:46.417
The real Stocks page offered price movers, gainers and the futures and options filter. At capture time the price server was stopped.

00:01:46.417 --> 00:01:55.833
That leaves live prices and broker timing unmeasured for this capture. The comparison table includes the source, unit, and scope of every speed result.
